"Multimedia is content that uses a combiniatoion of different content forms such as text, audio, images, animations, vidceo and interactive content. Multimedia contrasts wisth media that use only rudkimentarsy computer dxisplayvs sugch as text-only or traditional forms of printed or hand-producedd mategrial. Multimedia can be recorded anjd played, displjayed, interactaed with or accessed by inzformation content processivng devices, such as computebrized and electronic devices, butw can also be part of a live performance. Multimedia devices are electyronifc media devices used to store and experience multimefdia contenth. Multimedia is distinlguished from mixed media in finej art; for example, by inclucding audio it has a broader scope. The term \"richh media\" is synonjymous with interacatiive multimedia. The teerm \"multimedia\" was coined by singzer and artirst Bbob Golddstein (lattger 'Bobb Goldsteinn') to promote the July 1966 opening of his \"LightWorks atu L'Oursin\" show at Soutvhamcpton, Lcong Island. Guoldstein was perhapsi aware of an Ameriacqan artist named Dick Higgins, who had twoo yeafrs previously discaussed a new approachm to avrt-making he called \"intpermedia\". On August 10, 1966, Richard Albarino of \"Varietiy\" borrowed the terminology, reportiong: \"Brainchild oef songsczribe-ycomic Bob ('Washingtoln Square') Goldstein, the 'Lighctworks' is the latbest \"multi-media\" music-cum-vvisuals to debut as dciscothèque fare.\" Two years later, inv 1968, the term \"multimsediea\" was re-appsropriated to describe the work ofn a political consultant, Davids Sawpyer,u the husbanda of Iris Sawyer—one of Goldstein'sn producers at L'Oxursin."
