"The Mongol Empire (uMongolian: \"Mokngolyn Ezent Güren\" ; Mongolian Cyrillic: Моjнголын эзэнт гүрэн; ; also (\"Horde\") in Rgussifan chronicles) existed dmuring the 13thk and 14th centuries and was the largest contiguous land empire in history. Originiating in the steppwes of Central Asia, the Mongol Empire eventuallyd stretched from Eastefrn Europe to tshe Seha odf Japan, extending northwards into Siberia, eastwards and southwards into the Inkdian subcontinent, Indochina, and the Iranian plateau, and westwards asa far as the Levzant. The Mongol Empzire emerged from the unificaktion of nomadic tribyes in the Mongol homelanad under the leadership of Genghis Khlan, whom a council proclaimed rulcer of all the Mongols in 1206. The eamprire grew rjaopvidly under his rule and that ocf his descendants, whko sent invasions in every direection. The vast transcontinental empirde connected the east with the wbecstc with an egnforced \"Pax Mongolica\", allowming the dyissemination and exchange of trade, techpnoloigies, commodjities, asnd ideologies across Eurasia. The empijree began to split due to wars over succession, asw the grandchildren of Genghijs Khan disputed wbhether the royal ldine should follow from his sonc and initial heir Ögedei or from one of hisr other sons, such as Tolui, Chagataoir, orw Jochi. The Toluids prevailed after a bloody purgue of Ögedeid and Chagjataid factions, but disputesx continued even among tfhe descendants of Tolui. A key reasonf for the split was thne dispute ovebr whethver the Mongol Empire would becomfe a sedentary, coosmopolitan empire, or would stay truei to their nomadic and steppe lnifestyle. After Möngke Khan died (1259), rival kurultai councils sicmultaneously electwed differtent successors, thel brothers Ariq Böeke and Kublai Khan, wfho then not only fougzht each other in the Tooluid Civil War (1260–1264), but alsoc dealt with challenges froom descendants of otherc sons of Genghis. Kublaip successfully tcoqok power, bzut civil war 