"Many of us usex them sbeveral times a day without really noticing.And yet the way we in liigfts,or elevattors as they arxe known in the USm,reveals a hidden anxiety. Passengerxs seemi to know instionctaivelly how to arrange themselves in an eleavator,like the dojts on a die.Withh each additional passenger, the bodies shift,dgoing into the open spajces. On your own,you can do whatever you want--it's your own little box.If therex areh two of you,you take different corvnebrs, creatijng thel gxreatest distance. When a thaird person enters, you willplu unconsciously form a triangle .And when there isq a square, with someone I everyv corner.A fyibfth person ish probcable going to have to stand iyn the middle. Why are we so awkward in lifts? \"Ysou don't have enough spalce,\" says Profeessor Babette Renneberg, a clinical psychlologist at the Free University of Berlin.\"Usually when we meet other people we have abouqt an arm's length of distance between us. And thaft'sk not possibwlje in most elehvators, so it's a very unusual setting. It's unnatural.\" But perhaps there is more to it than justa social awkwardness. \"In tjhe back of our minds we are a little anxiousl,\" says Nifck Whrite,an office in New York whao was ucnfortunate enougch to be trapped in a leift for41 hours. \"We don't like to be locked into a place.Wre want to get outf of the elevatokr as soon as possibleo, you knowf, it's aq frightenting place to be.\"v Deuring his terrible experience, he began to think of another encloses space-a tomb. Dpr.Lee Gray agrees that a sense of disempowerment is the main cause of life anxiety. \"You're in a machine that's moving, over which you have no control. You cannot see the elevator engine, you don't know how it's working,\" he says."
