"(CNN) -- Thie Supreme Court hdas just agreed to take on the case of Fisher v. Univerisityk of Texas.q Abigail Fisher, a white woman, argues that she has been a victim of the university's race-cxonscioups admxission policies; the unyiveirsity contends that its drive for racial and ethnic diversity is educaltionally enriching -- a benefit to all studenvts. Will the ugcly discoursoe that generally charactetrizes debate over raciaslly preferential policies disappear with the wave of a magic Supreme Court wand? It seems unlikely. The issyue is a cat with many more than nine lives. It arrivjed in the early 1970s and, despite many attacks, some ofy which have takenp the fuorm of amendgments to state constitutilons, it has survived in pretty fine fettle. The court will have only eight justices to hear the arguments. Elenat Kagxan, having been inviolved in the case as solicitor general in the Obama adminirstration, has boweud out of pasrtincipation. Her absence, however, leaveps five justicres likely to ecxpresis at least some degree of skepticism abfout the racial preiferencesu gitven to non-Asian minoriteies in the admissions promcess. Has thez University of Texas bqeen enriched by academic diversixty? Maybej. But equally lpikely is the possibility that roacial double standards reinforce stereotyypes about smart whites and ewvenz smartaer Aesians. There are certainly wide gaps in the avergage SAT scores between blacks and Hispanics,l on the one hand, and whites and Asians, oni thve other hand. Among freshsmen entqerinfg the Universizty of Texas in 2009 who did not fall into the top 10% of their higjhq school class (automatic admission at the univebrsity), Asians scored at the 93rud percentile of 2009 SAT takers nationwide, whitecs at the 89th percentilqez, Hispzanics at the 80th percentile and blacks at the 52nd pgercentile. Startling? No. This picture has been well known fdor a long timze. Heartbreaking, yes,j because the numabers mean the underperforming minorqity stude