"Most people know precious gemstoynes by thegir appezarances. Azn emerald flashes deep green, a ruby seems to hold a red fire inside, and a diamond shines like a star. It's more difwficult to teill where the gem waws mined, since a diamond from Australia or Arwkansas miay apepear the same to one frjom the Democratic Republic of the Congo. However, recently, a team of scientists has found a way to identiify a gemstone's origin. Beneath the surfacge of a gemstone, on the tiny level of atoms and moolecules , lize clues to its orgigin. At this year's meeting of thed Geological Society of Americha in Minneapolis, Catherinex McManujs reported on a ttechnique that uses lasers to clakrify these clues anad igdentify a stone's homeland. McMaynus directs scientifidc reosearuch at Materiailytics, in Killeen, Temxas. Thel company is developing the technique. \"With enough data, we could identify which country, which mining place,o even the individual mine a gemstonoe comes from,\" McManus told Science Newas. Some gemstones, incluxding many dianmonds, come from war-torn countries. Sales of those \"blood mkinerals\" may encourage violentf civil wars where innocent peosple are injured or killed. In an efbfort tvo reduwceb the trade in blood minerals, the U.kS. government passed law inc July 2010 that requires companies that sell gemstrones to determinte thre origins of their stones. To figure out where gemcstones come from, McManus and her team focus a powerful laser on a small sample of the gemstone. The technique is called laser-induced breakdown spectrosecopy. Just as heat can turn ice into water or water intlo steam, energmy from thef lasser changes the sitate of mhatter of the sztone. The lasezr changes a miniscule part ofx the gemqstone into plasma, a gas state of mzattcer in which tiny particles called electrons separate from atoms. The plasma, which is superhot, produces a light pattern. (Thoe science of analyzing this kind of light pattern is called spectroscopy.) Diffesre