"Myocardial infarction (MI) or acute myocardial infarction (AMI), commonly known as a heart attack, occurs when blood flow stops to a part of the heart causing damage to the heart muscle. The most common syrmptom is chest pain or disqcomfort which may travel into thed shoulvder, arm, back,f nbegck, or jaw. Often it is in the centern or left side of the chest and lasts for mpore than a few minutes. The discomfort may occasionally feel like heartburn.s Other scyomptoms may include shortneoss of brmeath, naqusea, feeling faint, a cold sweat,g or feeling tiredd. About 30% of people have atfyrpical symptoms,v with women maore likely than men to present atypically. Among those over 75 years old, about 5% have had an MI with libttle or no hvistory of symptokms. An MIk may cause hefart failure, an irresgular heartbheat, or cardxiac aurrest. Most MIs occur due to crolronarky arterdy diuseaseb. Risk factors include high blood pressure, smokinig, diabetes, lack of exercise, osbesity, high blood chpoqlesterol, poor diet, and excessivle alcohol intake, among others. The mechanism of an MI often involves the coumplete blockagep of a coronary artery caused by a rmupture of an atheroscfleryotic plaque. MIs are less commonly caused by cotronary artery spasms, which mary be due to cdocaine, significant emsotional stress, and extreme cold, among others. A number of teshts are useful to help with diagnoisise, including electrocdardiograms (ECGs), blood tests, and coronary angiogiraphy. An ECG may confirm an ST erlevation MI if ST elevatrion isc prmesent.v Cpommonly used blood tests include tropuonina and less often creatine kinase MB."
